  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>The <strong data-start=\"267\" data-end=\"289\">fluid albumin test</strong> measures the concentration of albumin in body cavity fluids&mdash;most commonly <strong data-start=\"364\" data-end=\"381\">pleural fluid</strong> (lungs) or <strong data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"410\">ascitic fluid</strong> (abdomen). Albumin is a protein made by the liver and normally present in blood, but its level in fluids helps determine whether the fluid accumulation is due to a <strong data-start=\"575\" data-end=\"591\">transudative</strong> (low-protein) or <strong data-start=\"609\" data-end=\"622\">exudative</strong> (high-protein) process.</p>\n<p data-start=\"1187\" data-end=\"1277\">High albumin levels in body fluids typically indicate an <strong data-start=\"1244\" data-end=\"1265\">exudative process</strong>, such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1278\" data-end=\"1518\">\n<li data-start=\"1278\" data-end=\"1358\">\n<p data-start=\"1280\" data-end=\"1358\"><strong data-start=\"1280\" data-end=\"1294\">Infections</strong> (e.g., tuberculosis, pneumonia-related effusion, peritonitis)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1359\" data-end=\"1431\">\n<p data-start=\"1361\" data-end=\"1431\"><strong data-start=\"1361\" data-end=\"1375\">Malignancy</strong> (e.g., peritoneal carcinomatosis, pleural metastasis)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1432\" data-end=\"1471\">\n<p data-start=\"1434\" data-end=\"1471\"><strong data-start=\"1434\" data-end=\"1450\">Pancreatitis</strong> (in ascitic fluid)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1472\" data-end=\"1518\">\n<p data-start=\"1474\" data-end=\"1518\"><strong data-start=\"1474\" data-end=\"1496\">Pulmonary embolism</strong> (in pleural effusion)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1520\" data-end=\"1701\">Elevated fluid albumin reflects increased <strong data-start=\"1562\" data-end=\"1588\">capillary permeability</strong>, allowing proteins like albumin to leak into the fluid space&mdash;common in <strong data-start=\"1660\" data-end=\"1700\">inflammatory or malignant conditions</strong>.</p>",
